heduled tasks to return (default 20, max 100).", "type": "integer"}, "recurring": {"description": "When set, filters by schedule shape: true keeps only cron-driven (recurring) scheduled tasks, false only one-shot and fire-only scheduled tasks. Omit for both.", "type": "boolean"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_triggers"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "The documentation for the machine and product this session runs in: a claude.ai cloud container and the settings around it (GitHub access, connectors, the environment's secrets, network access, setup script and installed tools, the session's limits and permission prompts, Remote Control). Read it whenever something about your container or environment comes up, whether it blocked you, you worked around it, or the person asked how to set it up. For example: the repository the work is about is not in your container, a clone or push is refused, a service you need has no connected connector, an outbound host is denied, a command-line tool is missing, you need an API key, the person asks how to stop permission prompts. Read it rather than answering from memory because these settings move and get renamed faster than your training data, and a page says what is true now: the current steps, where in the product the person makes the change, and what you can do yourself. Reading a page also helps the person directly: in the Claude Code app they see a card with a button that takes them to that settings page, so they can fix it themselves while you carry on. Call it with no topic to list the pages and when each applies; call it with a topic to read that page. Read a page each time a different topic comes up; one read per topic is enough. It is read-only and needs no approval. It has nothing on bugs in the code you are working on.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__read_documentation", "schema": {"description": "The documentation for the machine and product this session